        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cytology—fine-needle aspirate of primary lesion may confirm diagnosis; however, ulceration, inflammation, and secondary infection may limit diagnostic utility.",
              "pdf_page": 1370,
              "printed_page": 1288,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Biopsy and histopathology—a deep wedge or punch biopsy is often needed to definitively diagnose squamous cell carcinoma. Multiple samples recommended as lesion may have a spectrum of actinic changes ranging from squamous metaplasia to invasive carcinoma.",
              "pdf_page": 1370,
              "printed_page": 1288,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cytology or histopathology of regional",
              "pdf_page": 1370,
              "printed_page": 1288,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Survival with radiotherapy alone (cats)— lymph nodes screening for regional metastasis mean 17.7 months; 1 year, 61.5% with (rare in cats, occasional in dogs). 81.8% recurrence.",
              "pdf_page": 1370,
              "printed_page": 1288,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Surgery (cats, nosectomy)—median survival",
              "pdf_page": 1370,
              "printed_page": 1288,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
